# Origin:  none -- Veil-authored. clearcotelabs/clearcote-browser's `147-media-capabilities` and
#          `148-media-devices` were audited as the counterparts (Veil/docs/MIGRATION-UNGOOGLED.md 6.5)
#          and neither is the source. Both read `ungoogled::CurrentPersona()` -- `.media_codecs` and
#          `.media_devices` -- which 6.9 rejected as a second identity source, and `147` also pins the
#          Worker/EME branch and `smooth`, which this patch deliberately does not. What transferred is
#          the set of call sites and two arguments, credited as arguments: substitute at the last
#          codec-aware point, and never over-claim a codec the table does not speak for.
# Source:  n/a -- authored in this repository.
# Commit:  n/a -- see this repository's git log for this file.
# License: BSD-3-Clause (see CREDITS.md), same terms as the code it edits.
# Changes: n/a -- not a port.
#
# THREE surfaces, one header, because they are three answers to one question and a page can compare
# them in three lines:
#
# 1. **`canPlayType` / `MediaSource.isTypeSupported`.** Measured on the shipping engine and on stock
#    Chrome 151: HEVC unsupported (`""` and `false`) on this Windows host, on a persona claiming an
#    Apple M3. Every Mac Apple has shipped since Kaby Lake decodes HEVC through VideoToolbox, so this
#    is the loudest single thing the codec block can say about the claim. Answered in
#    `media/base/supported_types.cc`, which is where all three JS entry points arrive, so one answer
#    cannot disagree with itself. Main and Main10 only.
# 2. **`mediaCapabilities.decodingInfo().powerEfficient`.** The GPU branch reports the *host* card's
#    hardware-decode answer verbatim, which is a GeForce GT 705 speaking next to an Apple M3 renderer
#    string. Replaced with the claimed part's: H.264 and HEVC hardware on every Mac, VP8 and VP9
#    software on Apple Silicon, AV1 hardware on the M3 and M4 families only -- keyed on the model
#    `011` picked for this seed, over `011`'s own table, so the two surfaces are two reads of one
#    machine. Seed 424242 selects index 5 of eleven, `M3`, which is the renderer string the committed
#    persona already carries.
# 3. **`mediaDevices.enumerateDevices()`.** Measured: one `audiooutput`, no microphone, no camera --
#    a server, which is what this host is. A laptop persona has all three kinds.
#
# THE DEVICE RULE IS ADDITIVE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN. An absent kind gains one described entry;
# a present kind is never replaced or trimmed. `getUserMedia()` resolves the device a page asked for
# out of this same enumeration, so substituting a described microphone for a real one would leave the
# user unable to make a call -- a Compatibility cost that the fingerprint gain does not justify, and
# the reason `148`'s wholesale replacement is not taken. Adding a kind the host lacks costs nothing
# that worked: capture there was already impossible. Same direction as `963`'s clamp-down and `967`'s
# refusal to raise a channel count.
#
# TWO ROWS THIS RE-SCOPES BY MEASUREMENT rather than by choice (6.15):
#
# - **`getSupportedConstraints()` gets no patch.** The inventory lists it as `969`'s. It is a fixed IDL
#   dictionary (`third_party/blink/renderer/modules/mediastream/media_track_supported_constraints.idl`)
#   whose only conditionals are three `[RuntimeEnabled]` features, and all three are decided by feature
#   state rather than by platform: `MediaCaptureBackgroundBlur` and `MediaCaptureCameraControls` are
#   `experimental` and `RestrictOwnAudio` is `stable`
#   (`runtime_enabled_features.json5:3990,3997,5152`). So 36 keys is what every desktop platform
#   reports, the measured 36 here included, and there is nothing platform-bound to move. Same shape as
#   `963` leaving `getContextAttributes()` alone once it turned out to carry no device identity.
# - **The main-frame/Worker `powerEfficient` split is preserved.** 6.6 recorded it as a decision T11
#   owed: stock Chrome reports `false` in a document and `true` in a Worker for the same supported
#   codec, because the Worker has no `gpu_factories` and falls to the perf-history branch. Measured
#   again here on stock Chrome 151 and on this engine, both realms, before deciding. This patch moves
#   the GPU branch only. Pinning both would make Veil *more* self-consistent than Chrome, which is its
#   own tell, and `smooth` is left on the perf-history value for the same reason.
#
# THE RESIDUAL, and it is a real one: this engine cannot actually decode HEVC on a Windows host, so a
# page that *plays* an HEVC stream fails where a real Mac plays it. Playing is a much rarer probe than
# asking, and a playback failure reads like a busy decoder or a mislabelled stream, where "no HEVC at
# all" reads as "not a Mac" from one line of JS. Recorded in 6.15 with the same standing as `906`'s
# advance-width residual: known, measured, and not fixable in the engine on this host.
#
# No switch is added. `--fingerprint`, `--fingerprint-platform` and the seed drive all three surfaces;
# `--disable-spoofing=gpu` opts the two codec surfaces out, which is the correct key rather than a new
# one: they are hardware-decode claims about the GPU `011` names, and with the GPU spoof off the real
# card is back and the claim would contradict *it*. The device roster has no key, because Veil has no
# field for it and a switch value nothing can emit is the same lie as a switch no Chromium has.
#
# `media/DEPS` gains `+components/ungoogled` and `media/base/BUILD.gn` the matching dep. That is a
# component the media layer may depend on: it holds the switch constants and the per-claim tables, and
# it depends on `//base` alone, so it cannot close a cycle. `media/base` already deps on
# `//components/system_media_controls/linux/buildflags`, which is the same shape.

